Essentially your in-window and out-window stores don't have to wait for data to commit, they just need the data-less coherence permission which coming from the
Home directory should be faster than the data which could come from DRAM (under loaded conditions)

Thus you have sped up fences and store-commits thereby being able to provide SC/TSO for cheap

Furthermore the paper talks about [18], coherence read-exclusive prefetch request which is allegedly in use by most x86 TSO implementations. This causes the
RFO request to be sent out early as soon as the store address is available.


